fllock -- the FreeLFS lock screen
=================================

A big clock, the date, your name and a password field on a plain screen. Super+L, the
panel's Lock button and closing the lid lock the screen.

The lock itself is the compositor's, not this program's (flcomp, fl_shell lock; see
src/flcomp/README "Lock screen"): while the screen is locked no other surface is drawn or
gets pointer or keyboard input, and no shortcut works except Ctrl-Alt-F1..F12 and the
media keys. fllock only asks for the lock, draws, checks the password and says unlock.
If fllock crashes the screen stays locked (solid colour); flcomp starts it again and, if
that does not help, shows "Screen locked. The lock program is not running. Press Enter to
start it again."

  fllock                 lock and wait for the password (Enter unlocks, Esc or Ctrl-U
                         clears the field, Backspace deletes)
  fllock --demo [--typed N] [--wrong|--busy|--stuck]
                         no lock request, a fixed clock: for screenshots (FLUI_PPM=FILE)

Password check: fllock-check (setuid root, group seat, mode 4750, set by the recipe's
owner= line; like flpower and flpkg-seat) reads one line from stdin and compares it with
the shadow entry of the real uid of whoever started it. fllock never runs as root and
never sees /etc/shadow. Exit codes: 0 right (or an empty password, as the login screen
allows), 1 wrong, 2 no password can unlock this account, 3 misuse. It takes no arguments and
reads no environment when privileged, so it cannot be used to try other accounts'
passwords; attempts take turns on /run/fllock-check.lock and a wrong one holds it for a
second, so guessing through it is one try a second however many copies run; no core dump;
the buffers are wiped. fllock sends the password through a pipe, wipes its own copy at
once, logs nothing, and waits for the answer without blocking the window ("Checking...").
There is no PAM on this system, so this is the same crypt() check flgreet-ui does.

If the account cannot be unlocked by a password (locked hash, expired) the screen says so
and how to get out: Ctrl-Alt-F2, log in, `pkill flcomp` ends the session.

Look: flui's theme (dark and light), the card-less layout of the login screen's family:
Plex Sans Light clock, an accent bar, sharp corners. Idle cost: one timer a minute.

Tests (make check, make e2e, host side, no root):
  tests/check.sh   fllock-check against a made-up shadow file (FLLOCK_SHADOW and
                   FLLOCK_LOCKFILE are honoured only when not root): right, wrong, empty,
                   locked, expired, other accounts' entries, over-long line, and the
                   one-try-a-second turn taking
  tests/e2e.sh     a headless flcomp, a window, Super+L, a wrong and a right password typed
                   through the fake keyboard, unlock (E2E_OUT=DIR keeps the screenshots)

Build: make; needs flui, wayland-client and libcrypt. PROTO= names fl-shell.xml (the recipe
copies it from flcomp). Install: /usr/bin/fllock, /usr/libexec/fllock-check.
